<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Duris of Samos</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 350–after 281 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Duris of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samo">Samos</a> was a Greek historian and tyrant of his native island in the early third century BCE. He wrote histories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> from 370 BCE, a history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agathocles-of-syracuse">Agathocles of Syracuse</a> and works on Samos, all now lost except in fragments. Ancient critics noted his dramatic, emotional style, and he is often seen as a leading example of 'tragic history'.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/duris-of-samos.jpg" alt="Duris of Samos" width="320" height="300" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ACMA_1333_Samian_decree_2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
